1
00:00:05,689 --> 00:00:07,820
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:07,821 --> 00:00:13,100
This time, let's learn about a method of adding shadows that brings out liveliness.

3
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:21,370
The reason I made a separate detailed lecture about shadows alone is that I value shadows very highly when I work.

4
00:00:22,725 --> 00:00:26,240
Shall we start by learning the definition of shadow?

5
00:00:26,241 --> 00:00:29,120
Here's the dictionary definition.

6
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:29,920
It's simple.

7
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:35,680
The part that appears dark because light is blocked and cut off by an object is called a shadow.

8
00:00:35,681 --> 00:00:39,001
Depending on the type of light, the appearance of the shadow can change.

9
00:00:40,285 --> 00:00:41,480
Let me give an example.

10
00:00:41,529 --> 00:00:43,320
You can largely divide it into three types.

11
00:00:44,855 --> 00:00:50,720
Light with direct travel, like sunlight, has a clear shadow boundary.

12
00:00:52,485 --> 00:00:53,600
We learned about that in Chapter 3.

13
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:57,080
This one.

14
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:05,370
For diffusely reflected scattered light, the shadow boundary can blur depending on how scattered it is.

15
00:01:05,371 --> 00:01:10,490
Sunlight that passed through clouds, or light reflected off a surface that undergoes diffuse reflection, are examples.

16
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:19,360
And third, if the object and the object the shadow lands on are far apart, the shadow can blur too.

17
00:01:21,529 --> 00:01:27,861
Even when there are multiple light sources, like a light fixture with several bulbs, they overlap and can blur.

18
00:01:29,534 --> 00:01:36,810
In the case of type two, you can't produce a clear shadow, so I don't use it much in my drawings.

19
00:01:36,811 --> 00:01:43,290
So I mainly use the characteristics of types one and three.

20
00:01:43,291 --> 00:01:47,290
In this lesson, you'll learn how to draw this shadow in actual practice.

21
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:52,540
What method should you use to add shadows?

22
00:01:52,541 --> 00:01:54,060
Let me draw a cube.

23
00:01:54,625 --> 00:01:57,740
Then decide the position of the light source.

24
00:01:57,741 --> 00:02:04,860
Imagine light coming from very far away, at a slight diagonal angle from the direct side.

25
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:08,501
Think of it as sunlight, very far outside the frame.

26
00:02:09,685 --> 00:02:14,780
Following the vertex where the shadow forms, draw the light's angle.

27
00:02:19,959 --> 00:02:24,410
And following the floor point, draw the light's direction.

28
00:02:26,109 --> 00:02:30,921
Mark a point where the direction line and the angle line meet.

29
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:40,851
And connect them, and you can indicate the shape of the shadow.

30
00:02:44,325 --> 00:02:49,360
What if the light comes not from the direct side, but a bit from the front?

31
00:02:49,361 --> 00:02:53,401
Draw the light's angle the same way, and draw the light's direction.

32
00:02:54,589 --> 00:02:59,481
The direction line passing through the vertices on each corner.

33
00:03:02,329 --> 00:03:05,660
And mark the point where the angle line meets.

34
00:03:18,415 --> 00:03:21,541
Connect them, and you can draw the shadow shape the same way.

35
00:03:25,665 --> 00:03:29,020
What if the light source is inside the frame?

36
00:03:29,021 --> 00:03:34,620
Drop a vertical line from the light source to determine its position.

37
00:03:34,621 --> 00:03:37,260
Mark the floor point like this.

38
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:41,461
Think of determining the height and position of a streetlight.

39
00:03:41,950 --> 00:03:45,461
Since the streetlight is behind, the shadow will be in front, right?

40
00:03:46,195 --> 00:03:48,300
Indicate the light source's angle.

41
00:03:52,829 --> 00:03:56,921
Since the light source is close, the angles aren't parallel.

42
00:03:57,910 --> 00:04:01,521
Draw them radially, starting from the light source.

43
00:04:02,549 --> 00:04:05,240
The direction is the same.

44
00:04:05,241 --> 00:04:06,760
Starting from the floor point.

45
00:04:08,149 --> 00:04:11,480
Draw them radially.

46
00:04:11,481 --> 00:04:15,441
Then mark points and connect them the same way.

47
00:04:16,305 --> 00:04:19,891
It's gotten a bit more complex, but it's drawable.

48
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:23,200
What if one more light source is added?

49
00:04:23,201 --> 00:04:24,960
Draw it the same way.

50
00:04:33,155 --> 00:04:34,590
Add one more streetlight.

51
00:04:34,591 --> 00:04:36,350
Draw the angles and directions.

52
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:44,681
Mark points, connect them, and it can be drawn like this.

53
00:04:46,070 --> 00:04:51,320
The part blocked by both light sources will be the darkest, of course.

54
00:04:53,975 --> 00:04:58,480
What happens if perspective is applied here too?

55
00:04:58,640 --> 00:05:04,160
A vanishing point forms for the light source, and a vanishing point forms for the shadow's direction too.

56
00:05:05,765 --> 00:05:08,160
My head really starts to hurt.

57
00:05:08,161 --> 00:05:11,321
And on top of that, we're doing this with a cube right now.

58
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:14,681
A character isn't made of cubes.

59
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:18,601
The clothing or body on which shadows form aren't flat planes either.

60
00:05:18,950 --> 00:05:22,800
Just because you've figured out this construction method, can you apply it to a drawing?

61
00:05:25,695 --> 00:05:33,690
This shadow construction method, of drawing the light's angle lines and direction lines that we've done so far, is completely useless in actual practice.

62
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:37,690
Then why did I draw all this?

63
00:05:37,691 --> 00:05:39,770
Well, it was to say that I at least know it.

64
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:46,590
In all my years of drawing, I've never once constructed shadow with angle lines like this.

65
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:52,030
Drawing shadows with construction on a character key art is practically impossible.

66
00:05:52,031 --> 00:05:57,550
For a background key art, maybe, but when drawing a character picture, I think it's completely worthless.

67
00:05:57,551 --> 00:06:01,950
Let me just touch on the theory briefly and get to the real subject.

68
00:06:01,951 --> 00:06:22,270
I especially find it useful to think of a picture as three-dimensional in my head when drawing shadows.

69
00:06:22,271 --> 00:06:23,790
Let me bring back the cube.

70
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:26,030
Then decide the direction of the light.

71
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:31,191
Then try making this into 3D in your head.

72
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:36,130
For explanation, I've visualized it in 3D.

73
00:06:38,110 --> 00:06:39,920
We learned the definition of shadow, right?

74
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:43,840
I said it's the part that gets dark because light is blocked by an object.

75
00:06:44,590 --> 00:06:48,361
Let me think about the blocked part of this cube.

76
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:50,400
When viewed from the direction the light comes from.

77
00:06:52,625 --> 00:06:56,770
The part of the cube that's hidden is the shadow.

78
00:07:00,270 --> 00:07:10,110
Coming back to the picture with this, I'll mark this hidden part.

79
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:29,320
The shadow is complete.

80
00:07:29,485 --> 00:07:31,320
You might feel this is hard.

81
00:07:31,321 --> 00:07:33,160
But it's not hard.

82
00:07:33,161 --> 00:07:38,680
The reason it feels hard is the thought of, can I really express that accurately?

83
00:07:38,681 --> 00:07:43,800
This method is, of course, far less accurate than when you constructed it.

84
00:07:43,801 --> 00:07:50,001
Especially the more angular and simple the shape, the more the shadow-forming part is flat, the less accurate it seems.

85
00:07:51,550 --> 00:07:58,720
In character key art, many shapes of various forms are freely tangled together, so even if it's not quite accurate, it's not a flaw.

86
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:04,451
I don't know if this'll be a good example, but when doing hard-surface rendering.

87
00:08:05,235 --> 00:08:13,900
Rather than this block-letter style, cursive is better. In block letters, if someone makes a mistake, it shows a lot, but in cursive, it shows less.

88
00:08:15,230 --> 00:08:19,360
As an example, I wrote something in card-style lettering.

89
00:08:19,361 --> 00:08:30,201
Swapping a few cards here, the block letters get weird, whereas this free-spirited writing doesn't show the gaps even after swapping a few cards.

90
00:08:32,890 --> 00:08:34,299
Think of it simply.

91
00:08:34,769 --> 00:08:41,100
It's fine even if it's off a little bit here and there.

92
00:08:45,405 --> 00:08:46,600
Really.

93
00:08:46,601 --> 00:08:48,520
At least, I've done it this way.

94
00:08:53,530 --> 00:08:54,780
Let me do one more.

95
00:08:54,835 --> 00:08:56,620
This time not with a cube.

96
00:08:57,850 --> 00:09:01,260
Let me do it with a sphere and a cone shape.

97
00:09:01,261 --> 00:09:02,540
Decide the direction of the light.

98
00:09:04,915 --> 00:09:08,380
Try making it 3D in your head.

99
00:09:08,381 --> 00:09:11,020
Let me show what that looks like in a drawing.

100
00:09:14,910 --> 00:09:20,000
There's a floor like this, and there's a sphere like this.

101
00:09:20,001 --> 00:09:21,600
And a cone shape.

102
00:09:26,270 --> 00:09:34,270
It'd be positioned like this.

103
00:09:34,271 --> 00:09:36,030
Now find the hidden part.

104
00:09:45,130 --> 00:09:52,970
Here, this part, and the floor surface.

105
00:10:07,130 --> 00:10:09,820
It can be expressed like this.

106
00:10:09,821 --> 00:10:11,260
Now if I paint here.

107
00:10:41,719 --> 00:10:42,720
The shadow will be complete, right?

108
00:10:42,721 --> 00:10:46,361
Let me do it once more, assuming the floor isn't flat.

109
00:10:46,450 --> 00:10:48,850
Use the same method.

110
00:10:48,851 --> 00:10:54,450
Imagine the view from the direction the light comes, grasp the shadow's position.

111
00:10:57,400 --> 00:10:58,400
Then draw.

112
00:11:18,280 --> 00:11:21,811
If I paint here, the shadow's complete, right?

113
00:11:38,645 --> 00:11:41,760
Even just seventy percent accuracy is good enough.

114
00:11:41,761 --> 00:11:47,920
What's the advantage of this method? You can look at the picture three-dimensionally.

115
00:11:47,921 --> 00:11:52,041
Because the step of forming it into 3D in your head is essential.

116
00:11:52,590 --> 00:11:57,120
Your ability to grasp form and create volume can only improve.

117
00:12:03,710 --> 00:12:06,190
Shall we get into the real thing?

118
00:12:06,191 --> 00:12:08,430
This time, I'll mark the shadow on a face.

119
00:12:08,431 --> 00:12:11,070
I've drawn a face for the demo.

120
00:12:11,071 --> 00:12:16,270
Constructing and drawing shadows on a complex face shape like this would be hard, right?

121
00:12:16,271 --> 00:12:19,751
I'll draw the shadow the way I recommend.

122
00:12:20,670 --> 00:12:26,080
After deciding the light direction, I drew the view from the direction the light comes.

123
00:12:30,085 --> 00:12:35,520
This view from the light direction, of course, there's no need to draw it in actual practice.

124
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:38,960
Because you can form it in your head.

125
00:12:39,010 --> 00:12:41,120
I drew it to explain.

126
00:12:41,121 --> 00:12:44,761
It's hard at first, but I really hope you'll master it.

127
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:46,160
It's really, really useful.

128
00:12:50,480 --> 00:12:59,481
Let me identify and mark the hidden parts.

129
00:13:30,449 --> 00:13:31,450
This side of the face is almost entirely hidden.

130
00:13:31,451 --> 00:13:32,451
Under the lip too.

131
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:41,320
Likely hidden.

132
00:13:41,321 --> 00:13:42,321
And this part here.

133
00:13:43,830 --> 00:13:46,760
You can see this part, so I'll mark it.

134
00:13:51,760 --> 00:13:59,360
The philtrum will be partially hidden too.

135
00:13:59,361 --> 00:14:01,440
This side of the lip is almost invisible.

136
00:14:07,760 --> 00:14:12,960
The opposite lip is invisible too.

137
00:14:12,961 --> 00:14:15,680
The lower lip will be hidden too.

138
00:14:30,510 --> 00:14:37,790
I'll also express the sternocleidomastoid here.

139
00:14:37,791 --> 00:14:38,791
Like this.

140
00:14:59,960 --> 00:15:07,960
You can see this part.

141
00:15:07,961 --> 00:15:09,960
And the other side is almost entirely hidden.

142
00:15:16,360 --> 00:15:24,360
For the neck, only this part is visible.

143
00:15:24,361 --> 00:15:26,040
This opposite side is hidden too.

144
00:15:29,100 --> 00:15:30,100
Right?

145
00:15:32,595 --> 00:15:35,740
The forehead will only be visible up to here.

146
00:15:38,750 --> 00:15:41,200
Like this.

147
00:15:41,201 --> 00:15:46,640
Now, just painting only these marked parts dark will make a surprisingly decent face come out.

148
00:16:52,510 --> 00:16:54,990
Let me duplicate and darken it.

149
00:17:09,444 --> 00:17:13,271
Now, even just merging this and doing some blending.

150
00:17:32,679 --> 00:17:33,680
It'll look decent.

151
00:17:33,681 --> 00:17:36,800
Now if I add more detailed value.

152
00:17:39,605 --> 00:17:41,070
The face rendering is complete.

153
00:18:18,274 --> 00:18:19,274
Not hard, right?

154
00:18:28,804 --> 00:18:32,160
Let me try once more with a different light direction.

155
00:18:43,339 --> 00:18:47,210
It's a commonly used light direction.

156
00:18:47,290 --> 00:18:49,210
Let's do it the same way.

157
00:18:59,204 --> 00:19:00,560
The light is coming from this side.

158
00:19:27,200 --> 00:19:28,200
If you do that.

159
00:20:57,669 --> 00:21:00,000
Duplicate and darken.

160
00:21:31,679 --> 00:21:32,680
Likewise, do some blending too.

161
00:21:32,681 --> 00:21:36,040
Let me also add some detailed value.

162
00:22:04,994 --> 00:22:07,710
Here, this part was missed.

163
00:22:07,711 --> 00:22:08,711
Let me add it in.

164
00:22:26,914 --> 00:22:35,390
You can add the face shadow easily.

165
00:22:35,391 --> 00:22:38,791
There's something to be careful about when expressing shadows.

166
00:22:39,100 --> 00:22:46,110
Assuming there's one light, even if the hidden parts overlap, you shouldn't draw the shadows overlapping.

167
00:22:46,111 --> 00:22:56,510
For example, adding the shadow caused by this box onto the shadow caused by a marble, the shadows shouldn't overlap like this.

168
00:22:58,999 --> 00:23:00,090
It should be like this.

169
00:23:01,584 --> 00:23:06,890
Also, you should refrain from drawing shadows using a multiply layer.

170
00:23:12,330 --> 00:23:20,330
Shadows aren't multiplied on top of light.

171
00:23:20,330 --> 00:23:24,490
Since we're not computers, being moderately accurate is good enough.

172
00:23:24,491 --> 00:23:30,611
Let me apply shadows to the picture we drew in Chapter 5, using what we've learned.

173
00:24:20,629 --> 00:24:24,241
I'll indicate the outline of the shadow with line drawing.

174
00:24:53,884 --> 00:24:57,761
I'll express the shadow in line with the indicated line drawing.

175
00:25:10,009 --> 00:25:13,820
I said I value shadows.

176
00:25:14,944 --> 00:25:18,341
Because I value light and want to handle it well.

177
00:25:20,189 --> 00:25:24,960
Just because you paint light into a picture doesn't mean light actually comes out.

178
00:25:24,961 --> 00:25:32,960
If your picture doesn't feel lit, don't paint light on using a dodge or layer property. Try finding the darkness instead.

179
00:25:34,459 --> 00:25:39,070
Just as darkness has presence because light exists, light has presence because darkness exists.

180
00:25:40,109 --> 00:25:43,671
You can know light exists because of shadow.

181
00:25:45,659 --> 00:25:51,070
When designing too, there has to be a simple part for the detailed part to shine.

182
00:25:51,071 --> 00:25:54,910
In a field of blue roses, a single red rose catches your eye.

183
00:25:57,344 --> 00:26:00,540
The elements of a picture are always relative.

184
00:26:00,541 --> 00:26:03,861
I hope you'll keep that in mind and work.

185
00:26:35,929 --> 00:26:38,590
I spent about ten minutes adding shadows.

186
00:26:39,069 --> 00:26:40,350
Let me compare.

187
00:26:43,254 --> 00:26:46,441
Even though I hardly spent any time, the three-dimensionality is more alive.

188
00:26:49,309 --> 00:26:52,880
We've learned about shadows up to this point.

189
00:26:52,880 --> 00:26:57,280
Next time, let's look at a method of drawing that brings out the material of the picture.
